{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "317",
    "case_summary": "A teenaged male presented with a 2-week history of vegetating, bleeding plaques and pustules on his face, scalp, trunk, and extremities. Lesions began as pustules and developed into painful, itchy plaques. Prior treatment included prednisolone tablet 5 mg twice daily, clindamycin hydrochloride 300 mg daily, and fluconazole 200 mg daily, but patient continued to develop new lesions. Patient reported difficulty walking due to skin lesions.\n\nPustules and hemorrhagic-crusted plaques were seen on the scalp, legs, arms, and trunk along with keloidal plaques on the face.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\nEnzyme-linked immunosorbent assay testing for HIV was negative\n\n- Histopathology:\nPunch biopsy from arm showed:\n- Epidermal hyperplasia and exocytosis of neutrophils\n- Dense dermal-mixed inflammatory cell infiltration\n- Microabscesses scattered with eosinophils\n- No granulomas or multinucleated giant cells\n- Negative periodic acid–Schiff staining\n\n- Images:\nImage A Title: Vegetative plaques on extremities\nDescription: Extensive vegetative plaques with bleeding and ulceration\n\nImage B Title: Lesions on extremities and trunk\nDescription: Pustules and ulcerated plaques\n\nImage C Title: Histopathologic examination (×100)\nDescription: Pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia (hematoxylin-eosin stain)\n\nImage D Title: Histopathologic examination (×400)\nDescription: Dermal neutrophilia and microabscesses with eosinophilia (hematoxylin-eosin stain)",
